# TypeScript Style

## Object (named) parameters over positional

Prefer a single destructured object parameter so call sites are self-documenting and order-independent.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — positional params
function createUser(name: string, email: string, isAdmin: boolean) { … }
createUser("Ada", "ada@example.com", true); // what is `true`?

// ✅ GOOD — named params
function createUser({ name, email, isAdmin }: { name: string; email: string; isAdmin: boolean }) { … }
createUser({ name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com", isAdmin: true });
```

Exception: single-argument helpers where meaning is unambiguous (e.g., `slugify(text)`).

## No `any`, no unnecessary `as` casts

- Never use `any`. Use `unknown` when the type is truly unknown, then narrow.
- Avoid `as` type assertions. Prefer Zod `.parse()`, type guards, or generics.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const data = JSON.parse(raw) as Order;
function handle(input: any) { … }

// ✅ GOOD
const data = OrderSchema.parse(JSON.parse(raw));
function handle(input: unknown) {
  const order = OrderSchema.parse(input);
}
```

`as const` and `as` in test fixtures where the type system can't infer are acceptable exceptions.
